[comment {-*- tcl -*- doctools manpage}]
[manpage_begin "Event log" n ""]
[copyright "2026, Ashok P. Nadkarni"]
[moddesc "Event Log"]
[titledesc "Commands relating to reading and writing the Windows event log"]
[require twapi_evt]
[description]
[para]

The [cmd twapi_evt] package implements commands for reading and writing to
the Windows Event log.

[section Sessions]

An event log (EVT) [emph session] is a connection to the event log service on
a system. Access to event log channels and event publishers takes place through
sessions. TclOO objects of class [uri #evtsessioncreate "EvtSession"] wrap EVT
sessions and provide factory methods for creating objects representing publishers,
channels etc.
The command below will create a [cmd EvtSession] object [cmd sess] for the
local event log using the default credentials of the current thread.

[example_begin]
EvtSession create sess
[example_end]

Options allow connection to remote systems and different credentials.

[para]
Session objects must be destroyed when no longer needed.

[example_begin]
sess destroy
[example_end]

This will free the underlying session handles as well as destroy any TclOO
objects created using its factory methods.

[section "Channels and file stores"]

Event log [emph channels] are named event streams storing event records
that can be read and written. The configuration properties for a channel are
managed through objects of the [uri #evtchannelconfig [cmd EvtChannelConfig]]
class created through the [cmd EvtSession]
[uri #createchannelconfig [cmd "createChannelConfig"]] or
[uri #newchannelconfig [cmd "newChannelConfig"]] methods.
The channels registered on a system can be retrieved with the
[cmd EvtSession] [uri #registeredchannels [cmd "registeredChannels"]] method.

[para]
Event records can also be stored in files by exporting them from a channel
with the [uri #exportevents [cmd exportEvents]] method. These can be read
back from the file using the same mechanisms as used for channels. Events
exported from a channel do not store message strings associated with the
events. To include these, the file must be further processed using the
[uri #archivelogfile [cmd archiveLogFile]] method.

[para]
Information about individual channels and file stores can be retrieved using
objects of class [uri #evtloginfo [cmd EvtLogInfo]] created by the
[cmd EvtSession] [uri #createloginfo [cmd "createLogInfo"]] or
[uri #newloginfo [cmd "newLogInfo"]] methods.

[section "Reading events"]

Events are read from channels and file stores by invoking
the [uri #getevents [cmd getEvents]] method on objects of class
[uri #evtreader [cmd EvtReader]] created using
[cmd EvtSession] [uri #createreader [cmd "createReader"]] or
[uri #newreader [cmd "newReader"]] methods. The event handles
returned are passed to rendering functions depending on the
event data desired.

[section "Rendering events"]

There are several options for retrieving the event data referenced
by event handles returned from [uri #getevents [cmd getEvents]].
The methods of the [uri #evtformatter [cmd EvtFormatter]] class
provides high-level access to event data. Objects of this class are
created by the
[cmd EvtSession] [uri #createformatter [cmd "createFormatter"]] or
[uri #newformatter [cmd "newFormatter"]] methods. The class takes
care of mapping raw event data to its publisher's metadata for
localized messages, levels, tasks etc.
[para]
Alternatively, the following lower level functions may be used. These
leave the caller responsible for retrieving associated publisher
metadata if needed.

[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [uri #evt_event_logpath [cmd evt_event_logpath]]]
Returns the channel or file from which the event was read.
[opt_def [uri #evt_event_render [cmd evt_event_render]]]
Returns event fields as a list based on a rendering context.
[opt_def [uri #evt_event_xml [cmd evt_event_xml]]]
Returns the event fields in XML format. Does not include rendered
messages. Use the [cmd EvtFormatter]
[uri #formateventasxml [cmd formatEventAsXml]] if that is
required.
[list_end]

The [cmd evt_event_render] command requires a
[emph "render context"] to be passed which selects the event properties
that are to be rendered from an event. The following commands create
a render context.

[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [uri #evt_render_context_system [cmd evt_render_context_system]]]
Returns a handle to a render context for system-defined fields.
[opt_def [uri #evt_render_context_userdata [cmd evt_render_context_userdata]]]
Returns a handle to a render context for publisher-defined fields.
[opt_def [uri #evt_render_context_xpaths [cmd evt_render_context_xpaths]]]
Returns a handle to a render context for fields matching
XPATH expressions.
[list_end]

[section Subscriptions]

Applications can register to be notifified of event arrivals through the
[uri #evtsubscription [cmd EvtSubscription]] class, instances of which are
created by the [cmd EvtSession] [uri #createsubscription [cmd createSubscription]]
or [uri #newsubscription [cmd newSubscription]] methods. Notifications may
be synchronous via the [uri #waitforevent [cmd waitForEvent]] or through
callbacks registered with [uri #registercallback [cmd registerCallback]].

[section "Writing events"]

The [uri #evtlog [cmd evt_log]] command writes events to the
[const Twapi-App/Operational] event log channel on the local system.

[section Publishers]
An event [emph publisher], or [emph provider] (the Windows SDK uses the term
interchangeably) defines the structure of events it may generate, the channels
to which it writes events, and localized message strings. This structure is
described by metadata in a publisher manifest that is installed on the system
or in [uri #archivelogfile "archived event files"] so it can be accessed without
registration on the system. The commands
[uri #evtpublisherinstall [cmd evt_publisher_install]] and
[uri #evtpublisherinstall [cmd evt_publisher_install]] respectively install
and uninstall publisher metadata in the local system.
[para]
The list of publishers registered on a system can be retrieved with the
[cmd EvtSession] [uri #publishers [cmd "publishers"]] method.

[para]
The [uri #evtpublisher [cmd EvtPublisher]] class encapsulates publisher metadata.
Objects of this class are created by the
[cmd EvtSession] [uri #createpublisher [cmd "createPublisher"]] or
[uri #newpublisher [cmd "newPublisher"]] methods. [cmd EvtPublisher] methods
can be then be used to retrieve various metadata for the publisher. For example,
the following prints the channels defined by Hyper-V.

[example_begin]
% sess createPublisher pub Microsoft-Windows-Hyper-V-Compute
% foreach chan [lb]pub channels] {puts [lb]dict get $chan -channelpath]}
Microsoft-Windows-Hyper-V-Compute-Admin
Microsoft-Windows-Hyper-V-Compute-Operational
Microsoft-Windows-Hyper-V-Compute-Analytic
[example_end]

Or to get the events defined by the publisher and the corresponding
localized message string in French (LCID 1036),

[example_begin]
% sess createPublisher frpub Microsoft-Windows-Hyper-V-Compute -lcid 1036
::frpub
% foreach event_def [lb]frpub events {-eventid -messageid}] {
    puts [lb]dict get $event_def -eventid]:[lb]frpub message [lb]dict get $event_def -messageid]]
}
...
500:Started DM operation add memory. Balancer requested to add %2 pages. (Virtual machine ID %1)
501:DM operation add memory completed %3 pages were added, time %2 s. (Virtual machine ID %1)
502:Started DM operation remove memory. Balancer requested to remove %2 pages. (Virtual machine ID %1)
...
1000:Le service de calcul hôte démarre.
1001:Service de calcul hôte démarré.
1002:Le service de calcul hôte est en cours d’arrêt.
1003:Service de calcul hôte arrêté.
...
[example_end]

Note above that localized messages depend on the appropriate locale being installed
and the publisher metadata including a localized string for the message id bound
to the event.

[section Bookmarks]
An EVT [emph bookmark] identifies a specific event in an event log. Bookmarks are
created with [uri #evt_bookmark_create [cmd evt_bookmark_create]] and
set to point to a specific event either by passing a
XML representation previously generated with
[uri #evt_bookmark_render [cmd evt_bookmark_render]] to that command or
with [uri #evt_bookmark_update [cmd evt_bookmark_update]] when given
a event handle. A bookmark may be used to resume reading events from its
targeted event by passing it to
[cmd EvtReader] [uri #seek [cmd seek]] method or for initializing a
[uri #evtsubscription subscription].

[section Commands]

[list_begin definitions]

[call [cmd evt_bookmark_create] [opt [arg XML]]]
Creates and returns a handle to an event bookmark. A bookmark identifies a
specific event in an event log and may be used to restart event processing from
that event at a later time.
If [arg XML] is specified, it must be the XML representation of a
bookmark as previously returned by
[uri #evt_bookmark_render [cmd evt_bookmark_render]].
[nl]
The returned bookmark identifies the event represented by the XML. If [arg XML]
is an empty string, the returned bookmark is not associated with any event and
may subsequently be updated using [cmd evt_bookmark_update].
[nl]
The returned handle should be released via [cmd evt_close].

[call [cmd evt_bookmark_render] [arg HBOOOK]]
Returns the XML form of a bookmark. This can then be passed to the
[uri #evt_bookmark_create [cmd evt_bookmark_create]] command to
recreate the bookmark to an event at some later time.

[call [cmd evt_bookmark_update] [arg HBOOK] [arg HEVT]]

Updates the bookmark specified by its handle [arg HBOOK]
to identify the event specified by the event handle [arg HEVT].

[call [cmd evt_close] [opt "[arg HEVT] [arg HEVT]..."]]
Close handles returned by various EVT API's. Primary purpose is to
close event handles returned by [uri #getEvents [cmd getEvents]].

[call [cmd evt_event_logpath] [arg HEVT]]
Returns the path of the channel or file from which the event
identified by [arg HEVT] was retrieved.

[call [cmd evt_event_render] [arg HEVENT] [arg HCTX]]
Returns a list of field values from event referenced by [arg HEVENT]
as per the rendering context referenced by [arg HCTX] which must be
a handle returned by
[uri #evt_render_context_xpaths [cmd evt_render_context_xpaths]],
[uri #evt_render_context_userdata [cmd evt_render_context_userdata]], or
[uri #evt_render_context_system [cmd evt_render_context_system]].
The order of values in the first two cases is the order of the XPATHS
in the render context and order of event's user data fields respectively. In
the third case, the order is always that of the
[uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_system_property_id "EVT_SYSTEM_PROPERTY_ID"]
enumeration in the Windows SDK.

[call [cmd evt_event_xml] [arg HEVENT]]

Returns the XML representation of the event referenced by [arg HEVENT],
including including system-defined metadata and provider-defined event data.

[call [cmd evt_log] [arg LEVEL] [arg MESSAGE] [opt [arg options]]]
Writes an event to the [const Twapi-App/Operational] channel on the local
system. The Twapi event provider should have been previously registered
on the system by some means such as the
[uri #evttwapiinstall [cmd evt_twapi_install]] command. Otherwise, logged
events are silently discarded by the system.
[nl]
[arg LEVEL] should be an integer or one of [const critical], [const error],
[const warning], [const informational] or [const verbose]. [arg MESSAGE]
is an arbitrary string. The message logged to the channel automatically
includes the path of the executable and the application name, which is
just [const "Tcl Application"] by default but can be changed by passing
the [cmd -application] option to the command.

[call [cmd evt_publisher_install] [arg MANPATH] [arg RESPATH] [arg MSGPATH]]

Registers an event publisher with the local system. [arg MANPATH], [arg RESPATH]
and [arg MSGPATH] are the paths to the publisher's manifest file, resource file
and message files respectively. The command may display an elevation prompt if
the process is not running in elevated mode.

[call [cmd evt_publisher_uninstall] [arg MANPATH]]

Unregisters an event publisher with the local system. [arg MANPATH] is the path
to the publisher's manifest file. The command may display an elevation prompt if
the process is not running in elevated mode.

[call [cmd evt_render_context_system]]
Returns a handle to a render context that can be passed to
[uri #evt_event_render [cmd evt_event_render]] to render the
system-defined properties of an event.
[nl]
The returned handle should be released using [uri #evt_close [cmd evt_close]]
when no longer needed.

[call [cmd evt_render_context_userdata]]
Returns a handle to a render context that can be passed to
[uri #evt_event_render [cmd evt_event_render]] to render the
user-defined properties of an event under the [const UserData]
section of the event, and the [const EventData] section if no
[const UserData] section is present.
[nl]
The returned values correspond to the properties defined by the
publisher in the event schema and returned in the same order.
[nl]
The returned handle should be released using [uri #evt_close [cmd evt_close]]
when no longer needed.

[call [cmd evt_render_context_xpaths] [arg XPATHLIST]]

Returns a handle to a render context for rendering the event properties
selected by the list of XPATH expressions passed in [arg XPATHLIST]
which must contain at least one XPATH expression.
The handle can then be passed to
[uri #evt_event_render [cmd evt_event_render]] to render the selected
property values.
[nl]
Each element of [arg XPATHLIST] specifies an XPath expression that
selects a [emph single] property from an event, for example
[const Event/System/Provider/@Name] or [const Event/System/Channel].
The values are returned in the
same order as the corresponding XPath expressions in the list.
[nl]
The returned handle should be released using [cmd evt_close].

[call [cmd evt_twapi_install]]

Registers the TWAPI event publisher with the local system. The command may
display an elevation prompt if the process is not running in elevated mode.

[call [cmd evt_twapi_uninstall]]

Unregisters the TWAPI event publisher from the local system.

[call [cmd EvtFormatter]]
Class for rendering and formatting events.
To guard against future implementation changes, objects of
this class should be created through
[uri #evtsessioncreate [cmd EvtSession]] methods
[uri #createformatter [cmd createFormatter]] or
[uri #newformatter [cmd newFormatter]] and not directly.

[call "[arg EVTFMTOBJ] [cmd decodeEvent]" [arg HEVT] [arg PROPERTIES]]

Returns a dictionary containing event data in fully decoded form.
[arg HEVT] is a handle to a single event. See the
[uri #decodeevents [cmd decodeEvents]] method for more information.
This method only differs in that it accepts a single handle and
return format is a dictionary.

[call "[arg EVTFMTOBJ] [cmd decodeEvents]" [arg HEVTS] [arg PROPERTIES]]

Returns a [uri base.html#recordarrays "record array"] containing
event data in fully decoded form. [arg HEVTS] is a list of handles
to events as returned from [uri #getEvents [cmd getEvents]].
The fields of the record array are those specified in [arg PROPERTIES]
which defaults to the list [const -providername], [const -eventid],
[const -level], [const -timecreated] and [const -message].
The table below shows the possible event fields that may be included
in [arg PROPERTIES].
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -activityid]]
GUID containing the activity id associated with the event.
[opt_def [cmd -channel]]
The channel to which the event was logged.
[opt_def [cmd -computer]]
The name of the system on which the event was logged.
[opt_def [cmd -eventid]]
The event id for the event's definition in the publisher's metadata.
[opt_def [cmd -eventrecordid]]
The id of this event record.
[opt_def [cmd -keywords]]
Contains a list of keywords with which the event is tagged.
[opt_def [cmd -level]]
The localized string associated by the publisher with the numeric level.
[opt_def [cmd -message]]
Contains the localized formatted message corresponding to the event. This
is similar to what is displayed in the Windows Event Viewer application.
[opt_def [cmd -opcode]]
The localized string associated by the publisher with the event opcode.
[opt_def [cmd -pid]]
The process id of the process that logged the event.
[opt_def [cmd -providerguid]]
The GUID of the logging event provider.
[opt_def [cmd -providername]]
The name of the logging event provider.
[opt_def [cmd -qualifiers]]
The high 16 bits of the event id for legacy event providers. This is combined with
the [cmd -eventid] field as low 16 bits to form a 32-bit id to retrieve messages.
[opt_def [cmd -relatedactivityid]]
GUID containing the relative activity id associated with the event.
[opt_def [cmd -task]]
The localized string associated by the publisher with the event task property.
[opt_def [cmd -tid]]
The thread id of the logging thread.
[opt_def [cmd -timecreated]]
The time the event was logged in 100ns units since January 1, 1601 UTC.
[opt_def [cmd -sid]]
The SID of the account under which the logging thread was running.
[opt_def [cmd -userdata]]
A list of the publisher-specific properties for the event.
[opt_def [cmd -version]]
The version of the event definition.
[list_end]

[call "[arg EVTFMTOBJ] [cmd formatEvent]" [arg HEVT]]
Returns the localized formatted message form of an event.
[arg HEVT] is a handle to an event.

[call "[arg EVTFMTOBJ] [cmd formatEventAsXml]" [arg HEVT]]
Returns event data in XML format. [arg HEVT] is a handle to an event.
Unlike the [uri #evteventxml [cmd evt_event_xml]] command, this includes
a [const <RenderingInfo>] XML element with the localized strings for the event.

[call [cmd EvtChannelConfig]]
Class wrapping a handle to a channel configuration information.
To guard against future implementation changes, objects of
this class should be created through
[uri #evtsessioncreate [cmd EvtSession]] methods
[uri #createchannelconfig [cmd createChannelConfig]] or
[uri #newchannelconfig [cmd newChannelConfig]] and not directly.
[nl]
Note that changes made to a channel configuration to not take effect
until they are saved.

[call "[arg EVTCHANCFG] [cmd access]"]
Returns an SDDL access descriptor that controls access to the channel and the backing
store in conjunction with the [cmd -isolation] property.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channeltype-complextype ChannelType.access].
[call "[arg EVTCHANCFG] [cmd bufferSize]"]
Returns memory buffer size for the channel in Kilobytes.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channelpublishingtype-complextype ChannelPublishingType.bufferSize].
[call "[arg EVTCHANCFG] [cmd clockType]"]
Returns [const 0] if using system time for time stamps and [const 1] for QPC.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channelpublishingtype-complextype ChannelPublishingType.clockType].
[call "[arg EVTCHANCFG] [cmd controlGuid]"]
Returns session GUID for a ETW session. Generally not present for event log channels.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channelpublishingtype-complextype ChannelPublishingType.controlGuid].
[call "[arg EVTCHANCFG] [cmd filePath]"]
Returns path of the backing log file. There may be environment variable
references in the returned path. These can be expanded with
[uri process.html#expandenvironmentvars [cmd expand_environment_vars]].
[call "[arg EVTCHANCFG] [cmd hasAutoBackup]"]
Returns [const 1] if a new log file is created when the current backing file reaches its
maximum size and [const 0] otherwise. In the former case, [cmd -retention] must
have a true value.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channelloggingtype-complextype ChannelLoggingType.retention].
[call "[arg EVTCHANCFG] [cmd isClassic]"]
Returns [const 1] or [const 0] indicating whether the channel is a classic (pre-Vista)
channel or not.
[call "[arg EVTCHANCFG] [cmd isEnabled]"]
Returns [const 1] or [const 0] depending on whether the channel is enabled or not.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channeltype-complextype ChannelType.enabled].
[call "[arg EVTCHANCFG] [cmd isolation]"]
Returns one of [const 0] (Application), [const 1] (System) or [const 2] (Custom).
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channeltype-complextype ChannelType.isolation].
[call "[arg EVTCHANCFG] [cmd keywordFilter]"]
If [const 0] or unset (empty), events are written irrespective of keywords. Otherwise,
only events that have a keyword included in the bitmask are written to the
channel.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channelpublishingtype-complextype ChannelPublishingType.keywords].
[call "[arg EVTCHANCFG] [cmd latency]"]
Returns milliseconds to wait before flushing buffers. If 0, buffers are flushed only
when full.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channelpublishingtype-complextype ChannelPublishingType.latency].
[call "[arg EVTCHANCFG] [cmd levelFilter]"]
Returns the severity level threshold for writing events to the channel. Events with
severity values above this are ignored. If [const 0] or unset (empty), all
events are written to the channel.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channelpublishingtype-complextype ChannelPublishingType.level].
[call "[arg EVTCHANCFG] [cmd maxBuffers]"]
Returns maximum number of buffers to allocate for the channel.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channelpublishingtype-complextype ChannelPublishingType.maxBuffers].
[call "[arg EVTCHANCFG] [cmd maxFiles]"]
Returns maximum number of times a new log file should be created when the channel is
enabled including reboots.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channelpublishingtype-complextype ChannelPublishingType.fileMax].
[call "[arg EVTCHANCFG] [cmd maxSize]"]
Returns maximum size of the log file.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channelloggingtype-complextype ChannelLoggingType.maxSize].
[call "[arg EVTCHANCFG] [cmd minBuffers]"]
Returns minimum number of buffers to allocate for the channel.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channelpublishingtype-complextype ChannelPublishingType.minBuffers].
[call "[arg EVTCHANCFG] [cmd name]"]
Returns name of the channel.
[call "[arg EVTCHANCFG] [cmd publisher]"]
Returns name of the publisher defining the channel.
[call "[arg EVTCHANCFG] [cmd publishers]"]
Returns list of publishers logging to the channel including the owning publisher that
defined the channel as well as those importing the channel.
[call "[arg EVTCHANCFG] [cmd retention]"]
Returns [const 1] or [const 0] depending on whether the backing log file is sequential
or circular.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channelloggingtype-complextype ChannelLoggingType.retention].
[call "[arg EVTCHANCFG] [cmd save]"]
Save changes made to the channel configuration.
[call "[arg EVTCHANCFG] [cmd setAccess]"]
Sets the SDDL access descriptor that controls access to the channel and the backing
store in conjunction with the [cmd -isolation] property.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channeltype-complextype ChannelType.access].
[call "[arg EVTCHANCFG] [cmd setAutoBackup]" [arg BOOL]]
If set to true,
a new log file is created when the current backing file reaches its
maximum size. In the former case, [uri #retention [cmd retention]] must
also be true.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channelloggingtype-complextype ChannelLoggingType.autoBackup].
[call "[arg EVTCHANCFG] [cmd setEnabled]" [arg BOOL]]
Sets the channel to be enabled if [arg BOOL] is true and disabled
if false.
[call "[arg EVTCHANCFG] [cmd setFilePath]" [arg FILEPATH]]
Sets the path of the backing log file.
[call "[arg EVTCHANCFG] [cmd setIsolation]" [arg ISOLATION]]
Sets the isolation level for the channel.
[arg ISOLATION] must be
one of [const 0] (Application), [const 1] (System) or [const 2] (Custom).
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channeltype-complextype ChannelType.isolation].
[call "[arg EVTCHANCFG] [cmd setKeywordFilter]" [arg KEYFILTER]]
Sets the keyword filter for the channel. If [const 0], event keywords
are not used to filter events.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channelpublishingtype-complextype ChannelPublishingType.keywords].
[call "[arg EVTCHANCFG] [cmd setLevelFilter]"]
Sets the severity level threshold for writing events to the channel. Events with
severity values above this are ignored. If [const 0] or unset (empty), all
events are written to the channel.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channelpublishingtype-complextype ChannelPublishingType.level].
[call "[arg EVTCHANCFG] [cmd setMaxFiles]"]
Sets the maximum number of times a new log file should be created when the channel is
enabled including reboots.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channelpublishingtype-complextype ChannelPublishingType.fileMax].
[call "[arg EVTCHANCFG] [cmd setMaxSize]"]
Sets the maximum size of the log file.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channelloggingtype-complextype ChannelLoggingType.maxSize].
[call "[arg EVTCHANCFG] [cmd setRetention]" [arg BOOL]]
Set to [const 1] if events are to be retained when file is at max size limit
or [const 0] if old events are to be overwritten.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channelloggingtype-complextype ChannelLoggingType.retention].
[call "[arg EVTCHANCFG] [cmd sidType]"]
Returns [const 1] or [const 0] indicating whether the SID of the logging principal
should be included in events logged to the channel or not.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channelpublishingtype-complextype ChannelPublishingType.sidType].
[call "[arg EVTCHANCFG] [cmd type]"]
Returns one of [const 0] (Admin), [const 1] (Operational), [const 2]
(Analytic) or [const 3] (Debug).
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channeltype-complextype ChannelType.type].

[call [cmd EvtLogInfo]]
Class holding information about an event log channel or file.
To guard against future implementation changes, objects of
this class should be created through
[uri #evtsessioncreate [cmd EvtSession]] methods
[uri #createloginfo [cmd createLogInfo]] or
[uri #newloginfo [cmd newLogInfo]] and not directly.

[call "[arg EVTLOGINFOOBJ] [cmd attributes]"]
Bit mask of the file
[uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/fileio/file-attribute-constants attributes].
[call "[arg EVTLOGINFOOBJ] [cmd creationTime]"]
Time the channel or file was created in units of 100 nanoseconds.
since Jan 1, 1601.
[call "[arg EVTLOGINFOOBJ] [cmd fileSize]"]
Size of the file in bytes. In the case of channels, the size of the backing store.
[call "[arg EVTLOGINFOOBJ] [cmd isFull]"]
Boolean indicating if the event log channel or file is full.
[call "[arg EVTLOGINFOOBJ] [cmd lastAccessTime]"]
Time the channel or file was last accessed in units of 100 nanoseconds
since Jan 1, 1601.
[call "[arg EVTLOGINFOOBJ] [cmd lastWriteTime]"]
Time of writing the most recent event to the channel or file
in units of 100 nanoseconds since Jan 1, 1601.
[call "[arg EVTLOGINFOOBJ] [cmd logName]"]
Name of the channel or file.
[call "[arg EVTLOGINFOOBJ] [cmd logType]"]
One of [const channel] or [const file].
[call "[arg EVTLOGINFOOBJ] [cmd oldestRecordNumber]"]
Record number of the oldest record in the file.
[call "[arg EVTLOGINFOOBJ] [cmd recordCount]"]
Number of event records in the channel or file.

[call [cmd EvtPublisher]]
Class wrapping a handle to the metadata of an event publisher.
To guard against future implementation changes, objects of
this class should be created through
[uri #evtsessioncreate [cmd EvtSession]] methods
[uri #createPublisher [cmd createPublisher]] or
