[call [cmd EvtReader]]

[uri #evtreader [cmd EvtReader]] class instances can be used to read
events from a result set returned by a query on channels or event log files.
To guard against future implementation changes, objects of
this class should be created through
[uri #evtsessioncreate [cmd EvtSession]] methods
[uri #createreader [cmd createReader]] or
[uri #newreader [cmd newReader]] and not directly.

[call "[arg EVTREADEROBJ] [cmd getEvents]" [opt [arg options]]]
Returns a list containing events from an event result set.
The returned list elements are event handles which can be rendered
using [uri #evtformatter [cmd EvtFormatter]] or commands
[uri #evteventxml [cmd evt_event_xml]] and
[uri #evteventrender [cmd evt_event_render]].
The event handles
must be explicitly closed via [uri #evt_close [cmd evt_close]].
They are not automatically closed along with the session.
[nl]
The command returns an empty list if no more events are available
in the result set. Additional events logged to the channel subsequent
to the query are not returned.
[nl]
The cursor in the event result set is moved past the returned events
so each [cmd getEvents] call returns events from the result set in sequence.
[nl]
The following options can be specified:
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -count] [arg COUNT]]
Returns [arg COUNT] events from the result set. The actual number of
events returned may be less if the end of the result set is reached.
If unspecified, the number of returned events depends on various
internal tuning factors and applications must not rely on a specific
number being returned.
[opt_def [cmd -statusvar] [arg VARNAME]]
The name of a variable in which to return status. If there is an
error when reading the next event, the command will normally generate
an Tcl exception. If this option is specified, the command will instead
return an empty list and store the error code in the specified variable.
[opt_def [cmd -timeout] [arg MILLISECONDS]]
Specifies the number of milliseconds to wait for events. A value of
[const -1] (default) indicates call should not time out.
[list_end]

[call "[arg EVTREADEROBJ] [cmd handle]"]
Returns the handle to the underlying EVT query.

[call "[arg EVTREADEROBJ] [cmd info]"]
Returns the status information for a query. For a structured XML
query, this is a list each element of which is a pair consisting of
a channel name in the query and a Win32 error code.

[call "[arg EVTREADEROBJ] [cmd seek]" [arg OFFSET] [opt [arg options]]]

Changes the position of the cursor from which events are read in the
wrapped result set by [arg OFFSET] records. A negative value will move
the cursor in the backward direction. A positive value will move it
forward. By default the offset is interpreted relative to the current
position of the cursor. The following options may be passed to the
method.

[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -hbookmark] [arg HBOOK]]
Causes [arg OFFSET] to be interpreted relative to the bookmark referenced
by the handle [arg HBOOK].
[opt_def [cmd -origin] [arg ORIGIN]]
Causes [arg OFFSET] to be interpreted relative to the current cursor
position, the first record of the result set or the last for [arg ORIGIN] values
[const current], [const first] and [const last] respectively.
[opt_def [cmd -strict]]
If specified, the command will raise an error if the offset or bookmark
seeks past the boundaries of the result set. By default, the cursor is
set to the position of the record at the boundary in such cases.
[list_end]

[call [cmd "EvtSession create"] [arg EVTSESSOBJ] [opt [arg options]]]
Creates a new TclOO object named [arg EVTSESSOBJ] encapsulating an event
log session. The command returns the fully qualified name of the
object.
[nl]
By default, the session is created for the local system
using the current process credentials.
The following options may be specified to instead connect to
a remote system or use different credentials.

[list_begin opt]
[opt_def -authtype [arg AUTHTYPE]]
Specifies the
[uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_rpc_login_flags "authentication method"]
to use for the connection.

[opt_def -domain [arg DOMAIN]]
Specifies the domain for the account specified by the [cmd -user]
option. This may be a Windows domain name or the name of the remote
computer for local accounts.

[opt_def -password [arg PASSWORD]]
Specifies the password corresponding to the account specified by the
[cmd -user] option. Must be in
[uri base.html#passwordprotection "encrypted form"].

[opt_def -system [arg SYSTEMNAME]]
Connects to the event log service on the system named [arg SYSTEMNAME]

[opt_def -user [arg USERNAME]]
Specifies the user account to use for the connection.
If omitted, the credentials of the current process are used.

[list_end]

[call [cmd "EvtSession new"] [opt [arg options]]]

Creates a new TclOO object with a unique name encapsulating an event log
session. Aside from generation of the object name, behaves identically
to [uri #evtsessioncreate [cmd "EvtSession create"]]

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd archiveLogFile]" [arg LOGFILE] [opt "[cmd -lcid] [arg LCID]"]]
Adds localized strings to the events in the previously exported event log
file [arg LOGFILE]. Option [cmd -lcid] specifies the locale to use and defaults
to the process locale.

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd clearChannel] [arg CHANNAME] [opt [arg options]]"]
Clears the specified channel. If option [cmd -backup] is specified, the channel
event records are backed up to the file whose path is given by the option value.

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd createChannelConfig]" [arg CHANCFGOBJ] [arg CHANNAME]]
Creates a TclOO object named [arg CHANCFGOBJ]
of class [uri #evtchannelconfig [cmd EvtChannelConfig]]
and returns its fully qualified name. The object wraps a handle to the
configuration information for the channel [arg CHANNAME]. The returned object
is automatically destroyed when [arg EVTSESSOBJ] is destroyed.

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd createLogInfo]" [arg EVTLOGINFOOBJ] [opt [arg options]]]
Creates a TclOO object named [arg EVTLOGINFOOBJ] of class
[uri #evtloginfo [cmd EvtLogInfo]] and returns its fully qualified name.
The object wraps a handle to the configuration information for a channel or event
log file. The returned object is automatically destroyed when
[arg EVTSESSOBJ] is destroyed.
[nl]
The channel or log file is specified through one of the following options.
The [cmd Application] channel is the default if no options are passed.
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -channel] [arg CHANNAME]]
The name of the channel to export. Must not be specified with [cmd -logfile].
[opt_def [cmd -logfile] [arg INFILE]]
The path to a event log file from which to read events.
Must not be specified with [cmd -channel].
[list_end]

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd createPublisher]" [arg EVTPUBOBJ] [arg PUBNAME] [opt [arg options]]]
Creates a TclOO object named [arg EVTPUBOBJ] of class
[uri #evtpublisher [cmd EvtPublisher]]
and returns its fully qualified name. The object wraps a handle to the
metadata for the publisher named [arg PUBNAME]. The returned object is
automatically destroyed when [arg EVTSESSOBJ] is destroyed.
[nl]
The following options may be passed to the command.
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -lcid] [arg LCID]]
The LCID for accessing localized metadata. The default value of `0` uses
the thread's locale.
[opt_def [cmd -logarchive] [arg LOGARCHIVE]]
Path to an [uri #archivelogfile archived] log holding the publisher
metadata instead of the default metadata installed on the system.
[list_end]

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd createReader]" [arg EVTREADEROBJ] [opt [arg options]]]
Creates a TclOO object named [arg EVTREADEROBJ] of class
[uri #evtreader [cmd EvtReader]]
and returns its fully qualified name. The object wraps a handle to
a EVT result set containing events from one or more channels or an
event log file. The returned object is
automatically destroyed when [arg EVTSESSOBJ] is destroyed.
[nl]
The following options may be passed to select events to be included
in the result set.
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -channel] [arg CHANNAME]]
Specifies the event channel from which events are to be read. Cannot
be used with the [cmd -file] option. Defaults to the [const Application]
channel if neither [cmd -channel] nor [cmd -logfile] is passed.
[opt_def [cmd -direction] [arg DIRECTION]]
If [arg DIRECTION] is [const forward] events are returned oldest
first. If [arg DIRECTION] is [const backward], events are returned
newest first.
[opt_def [cmd -logfile] [arg LOGFILE]]
Specifies the event file from which events are to be read. Cannot
be used with the [cmd -channel] option.
[opt_def [cmd -ignorequeryerrors]]
Normally, if retrieving a specific event results in an error, Windows
will fail the query and a Tcl exception will be raised.
If [cmd -ignorequeryerrors] is specified, such events are simply skipped
and any further events are returned.
[opt_def [cmd -query] [arg QUERY]]
Specifies the selection criteria for events that should be included in the
returned result set.
[arg QUERY] should be the empty string or [const *] (for all events, default),
an XPATH expression or a structured XML query. See
[uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/consuming-events "SDK documentation"].
[list_end]

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd createSubscription]" [arg EVTSUBSCRIBEOBJ] [arg CHANNAME] [opt [arg options]]]
Creates a TclOO object named [arg EVTSUBSCRIBEOBJ] of class
[uri #evtsubscription [cmd EvtSubscription]]
and returns its fully qualified name. The object wraps a handle to an
EVT event subscription containing events from one or more channels. The
returned object is automatically destroyed when [arg EVTSESSOBJ] is destroyed.
[nl]
The [arg CHANNAME] argument specifies the name of the channel. Pass an empty
string if and only if the [cmd -query] option value is a structured XML query.
[nl]
The following options may be passed to select events to be included
in the result set.
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -hbookmark] [arg HBOOKMARK]]
Handle to a bookmark. If specified, all events after the bookmark
that match the query are included in the subscription.
[opt_def [cmd -ignorequeryerrors]]
Normally, if retrieving a specific event results in an error, Windows
will fail the query and a Tcl exception will be raised.
If this option is specified, such events are simply skipped
and any further events are returned.
[opt_def [cmd -includeexisting]]
By default the command will only subscribe to new events. If this option
is specified, notification is sent for existing events as well.
[opt_def [cmd -query] [arg QUERY]]
Specifies the selection criteria for events that should be included in the
returned result set.
[arg QUERY] should be an empty string or [const *] (for all events, default),
an XPATH expression or a structured XML query. See
[uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/consuming-events "SDK documentation"].
[opt_def [cmd -strict]]
If specified, an error is raised if the event referenced by passed bookmark
does not exist.
[list_end]

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd createFormatter]" [arg EVTFMTOBJ] [opt [arg options]]]
Creates a TclOO object named [arg EVTFMTOBJ] of class
[uri #evtformatter [cmd EvtFormatter]] and returns its fully qualified name.
The object is automatically destroyed when [arg EVTSESSOBJ] is destroyed.
[nl]
The following options may be passed to the command.
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -lcid] [arg LCID]]
The LCID for accessing localized metadata. The default value of [const 0] uses
the thread's locale.
[opt_def [cmd -logarchive] [arg LOGARCHIVE]]
Path to an [uri #archivelogfile archived] log holding the publisher
metadata instead of the default metadata installed on the system.
[list_end]

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd destroy]"]
Calling the [cmd destroy] method on a [cmd EvtSession] object will
close the wrapped Win32 EVT session handle. Any objects created, such as
[cmd EvtPublisher], [cmd EvtChannel] etc., through its factory
methods will also be destroyed.

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd exportEvents]" [arg OUTFILE] [arg options]]
Exports events from a channel, logfile or a query to the file [arg OUTFILE]
which is overwritten. The following options may be specified.

[list_begin opt]
[opt_def -channel [arg CHANNAME]]
The name of the channel to export. Must not be specified with [cmd -logfile].
[opt_def -logfile [arg INFILE]]
The path to a event log file from which to read events.
Must not be specified with [cmd -channel].
[opt_def -query [arg QUERY]]
An XPath or structured XML query to select events to include in the export.
A value of [const *] or the empty string selects all events.
By default, all events are exported.
[opt_def -ignorequeryerrors [arg BOOL]]
If set to true, any errors in the query are ignored. Default is false.
[list_end]

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd handle]"]
Returns the wrapped Win32 EVT session handle.

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd isLocal]"]
Return 1 if the session wrapped by the [arg SESSOBJ] object is the default local
session and 0 otherwise.

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd newChannelConfig]" [arg CHANNAME]]
Creates and returns a uniquely named TclOO object of class
[uri #evtchannelconfig [cmd EvtChannelConfig]].
The object wraps a handle to the
configuration information for the channel [arg CHANNAME]. The returned object
is automatically destroyed when [arg EVTSESSIONOBJ] is destroyed.

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd newLogInfo]" [opt [arg options]]]
Creates and returns a uniquely named TclOO object of class
[uri #evtloginfo [cmd EvtLogInfo]]. The object wraps a handle to the
configuration information for a channel or event log file.
The returned object is automatically destroyed when [arg EVTSESSIONOBJ]
is destroyed. See [uri #createloginfo [cmd createLogInfo]] for options.

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd newPublisher]" [arg PUBNAME] [opt [arg options]]]
Creates and returns a uniquely named TclOO object of class
[uri #evtpublisher [cmd EvtPublisher]]. The object wraps a handle to the
metadata for the publisher named [arg PUBNAME]. The returned object is
automatically destroyed when [arg EVTSESSIONOBJ] is destroyed. See
[uri #createpublisher [cmd createPublisher]] for options.

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd newReader]" [opt [arg options]]]
Creates and returns a uniquely named TclOO object of class
[uri #evtreader [cmd EvtReader]]. The object wraps a handle to the
a EVT result set containing events from one or more channels or an
event log file. The returned object is
automatically destroyed when [arg EVTSESSIONOBJ] is destroyed. See
[uri #createreader [cmd createReader]] for options.

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd newSubscription]" [opt [arg options]]]
Creates and returns a uniquely named TclOO object of class
[uri #evtsubscription [cmd EvtSubscription]]. The object wraps a handle to the
EVT event subscription containing events from one or more channels. The
returned object is automatically destroyed when [arg EVTSESSOBJ] is destroyed.
See [uri #createsubscription [cmd createSubscription]] for options.

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd newFormatter]" [opt [arg options]]]
Creates and returns a uniquely named TclOO object of class
[uri #evtformatter [cmd EvtFormatter]].
The object is automatically destroyed when [arg EVTSESSOBJ] is destroyed.
See [uri #createformatter [cmd createFormatter]] for options.

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd publishers]"]
Returns a list of the names of event publishers registered on
the system connected by the session.

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd registeredChannels]"]
Returns a list of channel names registered on the system.

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd with]" [arg FACTORY] [arg TARGETMETHOD]]
This is a convenience method to create a Evt object belonging to the
session with the [arg FACTORY] method, invoking the created object's
[arg TARGETMETHOD] method and then destroying it. The command
returns the result of the [arg TARGETMETHOD] invocation.

[call [cmd EvtSubscription]]

Class for subscribing to events.
To guard against future implementation changes, objects of
this class should be created through
[uri #evtsessioncreate [cmd EvtSession]] methods
[uri #createsubscription [cmd createSubscription]] or
[uri #newsubscription [cmd newSubscription]] and not directly.

[call "[arg EVTSUBSCRIBEOBJ] [cmd registerCallback]" [arg CALLBACK] [opt [arg MILLISECS]]]

Registers a callback to be invoked when an event is available on the
subscribed channels. Any previously registered callback is replaced.
[arg MILLISECS] defaults to [const -1] indicating no timeout. Otherwise
the callback is invoked after [arg MILLISECS] irrespective of whether
an event is available.
The callback should call [uri #getEvents [cmd getEvents]] to retrieve
new events but be prepared to handle the case that no events have
actually arrived.

[call "[arg EVTSUBSCRIBEOBJ] [cmd unregisterCallback]"]

Unregisters callback previously registered with
[uri #registercallback [cmd registerCallback]].

[call "[arg EVTSUBSCRIBEOBJ] [cmd waitForEvents] [opt [arg MILLISECS]]"]

Waits for [arg MILLISECS] till an event is available on the subscribed
channels. [arg MILLISECS] defaults to [const -1] indicating no timeout.
On return, the caller should call [uri #getEvents [cmd getEvents]]
but be prepared to handle the case that no events have actually arrived.
[nl]
The method should not be called on subscriptions where a callback has
been registered.

[list_end]


